>> title: Cuban Black Beans, Instant Pot
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: beans, cuban, sofrito, instant pot, pressure cooker, no soak
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot black beans, pressure cooker black beans, no soak black beans, instant pot frijoles negros, frijoles negros, Cuban black beans
>> servings: 6
>> dish: cuban-black-beans
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> time: 1 hr 35 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the release has to be waited out, because venting a bean pot splits every skin on the way down and nothing re-closes them
>> step.1: fry the sofrito on Sauté 10 min
>> step.2: toast the spices 1 min
>> step.3: cook the beans from dry — 30 min at high pressure
>> step.4: wait out the natural release 20 min
>> step.5: reduce lid off 10 min, then the wine and vinegar

Fry @green bell pepper{1%large}, @yellow onion{1%large} and @garlic{5%cloves}(all chopped) in @olive oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL) in the #Instant Pot{} on Sauté ~fry{10%min}, until the onion is soft and going gold at the edges. Same pot, no second pan — the sofrito is why the beans taste of anything, and the pot browning it itself is the whole claim of the appliance.

Toast @&(~1)sofrito{} with @ground cumin{1 1/2%tsp} and @dried oregano{1%tsp} ~toast{1%min}, until it smells like the spices and not like the jar.

Tip @dried black beans{1%lb}(450 g; dry, not soaked) and @water{6%cups}(1.4 L) onto @&(~1)sofrito{} with @bay leaves{2}, scrape the bottom of the pot clean, lock the lid and ~pressure cook{30%min} at high pressure. Thirty minutes from dry is the black bean's own number and it has nothing to do with the ninety-minute stovetop simmer — that one is counted from an overnight soak, and there is no soak here. Six cups of water rather than eight, because nothing evaporates under a lid.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{20%min} until the pin drops by itself. A quick release splits black beans along their skins and you get a pot of grey water with the beans still in pieces in it.

Reduce @&(~1)beans{} on Sauté with the lid off ~reduce{10%min} until the broth is thick, then stir in @dry white wine{2%Tbs}, @cider vinegar{1%Tbs} and @kosher salt{2%tsp} and pull the bay leaves. The acid goes in at the end, not at the start: vinegar and wine in with dry beans set the skins and no amount of extra time fixes it. The lid-off reduce is doing the job the stovetop version's last half hour does — a sealed pot boils nothing away, so every one of these comes out looser than you expect.
